#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# n_dsolve_value

#% text
# `n_dsolve_value(eqs, expr, [t, t0, t1])` solves the differential equations `eqs` numerically and gives `expr`, an expression in `y(t)`, `y'(t)`, ..., along the solution.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For an `expr` that depends on `t` the result is a function of `t`; for `expr = y(t1)` and other values at a fixed time it is a number.

#% item
# The options are those of `n_dsolve`: `method`, `digits`, `events` and so on.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == -y(t), y(0) == 1], y(1), [t, 0, 1])
#% output
#= 0.3678794422457897

#% cell
f = n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == -y(t), y(0) == 1], y(t), [t, 0, 2]); f(0.5)
#% output
#= 0.6065306602814926

#% cell
n_dsolve_value([y''(t) + y(t) == 0, y(0) == 0, y'(0) == 1], y(2), [t, 0, 2])
#% output
#= 0.9092974217084951

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# An expression in the solution and its derivative, the energy of an oscillator:

#% cell
en = n_dsolve_value([y''(t) + y(t) == 0, y(0) == 1, y'(0) == 0], y(t)^2 + y'(t)^2, [t, 0, 10]); [en(0.0), en(5.0), en(10.0)]
#% output
#= [1.0, 0.9999999711997185, 0.999999939853417]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# 25 digits:

#% cell
n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == y(t), y(0) == 1], y(1), [t, 0, 1], digits: 25)
#% output
#= 2.7182818284590452353602875

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The exact solution:

#% cell
exp(-1.0)
#% output
#= 0.36787944117144233

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n_dsolve`, `parametric_n_dsolve_value`, `interpolating_function`
